### 3. DESIGN SYSTEM

Use a centralized design token architecture.

All components must inherit their visual properties from the same design system.

#### 3.1 Light Color Palette

Use the following exact reference colors:

| Token | Hex | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Canvas | #F7F7F4 | Main application background |
| Surface | #FFFFFF | Cards and panels |
| Ink | #17191F | Primary text |
| Muted | #68717E | Secondary text |
| Primary | #5268D8 | Primary actions and highlights |
| Success | #21A68A | Successful states |
| Warning | #E4A44A | Warnings and review states |
| Danger | #DC6470 | Errors and critical states |
| Border | #E7E9ED | Borders and dividers |

#### 3.2 Dark Color Palette

Use the following exact reference colors:

| Token | Hex | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| Canvas | #111318 | Main background |
| Surface | #1B1E25 | Cards and panels |
| Elevated | #292E38 | Elevated surfaces |
| Ink | #E9EBF0 | Primary text |
| Muted | #9BA3B2 | Secondary text |
| Primary | #8292F0 | Primary actions |

Dark mode must preserve the same component hierarchy and layout as light mode.

Do not simply invert colors. Each surface must use the appropriate dark palette token.

#### 3.3 Typography System

Use exactly three primary font families:

**Manrope**
- Page headings.
- Section headings.
- KPI values.
- Important numerical information.
- Editorial emphasis.

**Inter**
- Body text.
- Navigation labels.
- Form controls.
- Descriptions.
- General UI content.

**JetBrains Mono**
- Timestamps.
- IDs.
- Metadata.
- Metrics.
- Technical labels.
- Status information.
- Small uppercase section identifiers.

Typography rules:
- Use a strong hierarchy between page titles, section titles, body text, and metadata.
- Maintain consistent font weights.
- Use compact tracking for editorial headings.
- Use monospaced typography for analytical and technical information.
- Avoid using multiple font families within the same text element.
- Never use typography as decoration.

#### 3.4 Border Radius

Use a controlled radius scale:

- 6px: Small controls and compact elements.
- 8px: Inputs and buttons.
- 12px: Standard cards.
- 16px: Large panels.
- 20px: Special elevated or floating surfaces.

Do not randomly introduce other radius values.

#### 3.5 Spacing System

Follow an 8-point spacing scale.

Primary spacing values:
- 4px
- 8px
- 16px
- 24px
- 32px
- 48px

Use 4px only for fine adjustments.

Maintain consistent horizontal and vertical spacing across all pages.

#### 3.6 Borders and Shadows

- Use subtle 1px borders for card separation.
- Prefer borders over heavy shadows.
- Use soft shadows only for floating interfaces, overlays, and elevated elements.
- Avoid unnecessary visual depth.
- Maintain clear separation between adjacent surfaces.

